This overlay is a teaching artifact, not a deployment.

The file's presence is the whole declaration; nothing reads its contents. Two
things act on it:

  * `make up` leaves this overlay out of auto-discovery, so adding it here can
    never change which real deployment a bare `make up` starts, and gives it a
    backend-only stack with no Postgres, no accounts and no external volume.

  * The backend's overlay discovery does not count it as this deployment's own,
    so a checkout with one real overlay still resolves to that one.

It is a file rather than a line in deploy/backend.env because that file is a
dotenv read by Compose, and Compose's parser is not ours: it accepts
`DISTRIBUTION_KIND = example`, with spaces, as the value `example`, while a
grep for the exact assignment does not. An overlay that reads as a teaching
artifact to Compose and as a deployment to Make is the one combination that
creates production volumes for the tutorial. Existence has no second reading.

The public export reaches the same conclusion by a different route: its
manifest names this directory under `keep:`. That is deliberate. A publication
boundary should be a curated list somebody signs off on, not a flag an overlay
can set for itself.
